  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 313 PM EST MON NOV 9 2026\n\nThe long term period will begin with significant changes in progress \nover the eastern CONUS. Most of Wednesday won't seem much different \nin terms of perceptible weather, but increasing clouds will be a \nsign.  A shortwave trough moving onshore in the Pacific Northwest \nwill advance to the Midwest by Wednesday morning, supporting a \ndeveloping surface low there. This shortwave will get picked up by a \nlarger trough dropping south out of Ontario, which then evolves into \na very deep upper trough over the eastern CONUS. This supports \nfurther deepening of the surface low, with eventual coastal \ndevelopment likely as it shifts east. Models are coming into better \nagreement for the surface low to track by just our north through the \nOhio Valley on Wednesday night, with the system's cold front passing \nthrough our area sometime around dawn on Thursday. Although it \ndoesn't look like significant gulf moisture will make it here ahead \nof the system, the system's strength should overcome the lack of \nmoisture, with rain breaking out anyway as the low and its cold \nfront approach. \n\nAs the intense and still deepening low departs to the east, its \npowerful circulation will pull much colder air southward over our \narea, likely resulting in steady or falling temperatures during the \nday Thursday. The initial deep moisture with the approach of the low \nand cold front will also depart to the east, but shallower low level \nmoisture will linger in the upslope cold air advection, supporting \nthe potential for light precip at times, especially in the upslope \nfavored area of southeast KY. It may become cold enough for the \nremaining rain to mix with or change to snow. The 12Z runs of the \nGFS and ECMWF have come into better agreement for cooling, now \nindicating the lower atmosphere becoming marginally cold enough for \nsnow (1000-850mb thickness of ~1300) Friday morning. There is also \nan interesting feature in the form a shortwave trough approaching \nfrom the northwest Friday afternoon or evening with a possible \nenhancement of deep moisture in the cold air, as well as a potential \nmoisture plume off of Lake Michigan. However, won't get too excited \nover these features yet, since it is still four days out and easily \nsubject to change in subsequent runs. The official forecast at this \ntime will still keep any snow accumulations from the system very \nlight and mainly confined to the highest elevations near the VA \nborder.\n\nSurface ridging eventually builds in from the west during the \nweekend and then persists into Monday, as the deep upper trough also \npulls out to the east. This will eventually lead to clearing, but \nthe timing is uncertain. The official forecast may be a bit too fast \nto break up clouds on Friday night and Saturday.",
